WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.980
Deviations are departures from approved procedures, established processes or expected conditions.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:13.160
A robust pharmaceutical quality system encourages prompt reporting and proportionate investigation.

3
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.960
The purpose is to understand what happened, assess potential product impact

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.990
and prevent recurrence. Corrective and preventive action, or C A P

5
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.880
A, is one possible output of a documented investigation.

6
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:32.420
When an unexpected event is detected, personnel should first protect people,

7
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.720
product and evidence as appropriate to the situation. This may require

8
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.420
stopping an activity, segregating affected materials or placing a batch on

9
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:47.500
hold under approved procedures. Immediate containment controls the potential consequences while

10
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.820
the quality assessment continues. Personnel must not silently correct or conceal

11
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.460
an event.

12
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.140
Deviation records should capture what happened, when and where it occurred,

13
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.880
the affected equipment or materials, and the immediate actions taken. Classification

14
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.420
and investigation depth should reflect risk, potential impact and site procedures.

15
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:16.740
Records should distinguish observed facts from assumptions. Clear contemporaneous documentation supports

16
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.480
reliable evaluation and later review.

17
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.700
Root cause investigation seeks to explain why a deviation occurred, not

18
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:29.280
simply identify who was present. Teams may use a timeline, process

19
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.660
mapping, the five whys or a cause-and-effect diagram when suitable. Potential

20
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.700
causes should be tested against available evidence. Human error alone is

21
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:45.140
rarely an adequate explanation if procedure design, training, equipment or working

22
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.380
conditions contributed.

23
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:53.560
Product impact assessment considers the affected materials, batches, processes, time window

24
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.800
and available supporting data. The assessment should be scientifically justified and

25
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.780
documented by authorized personnel. It may require additional investigation or testing,

26
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:09.060
but testing alone cannot always establish that a process deviation had

27
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:15.170
no impact. Final disposition follows applicable procedures and regulatory requirements.

28
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.350
A correction addresses an identified problem, while a corrective action aims

29
00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:25.090
to remove its cause and prevent recurrence. Preventive actions may address

30
00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:30.050
potential problems before they occur. Actions should have clear ownership, due

31
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:35.450
dates and documented implementation. Retraining may be appropriate in some cases,

32
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:39.030
but should not be the default response without understanding the underlying

33
00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:39.660
cause.

34
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.520
CAPA effectiveness should be evaluated using measures suited to the identified

35
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.920
cause and risk. Examples include observing process performance, reviewing repeat events

36
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.220
or verifying that a new control works as intended. Trend analysis

37
00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:01.580
can identify recurring deviations that individual investigations miss. An action should

38
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.530
not be declared effective solely because it was completed on schedule.

39
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.550
Imagine a filling-line differential pressure alarm that occurred during production and

40
00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.050
was not promptly documented. The event should be reported, relevant records

41
00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:20.210
preserved and the affected period assessed. Authorized personnel should investigate the

42
00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:25.550
alarm, the response and potential product impact. CAPA, if needed, should

43
00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.330
address the demonstrated causes, with effectiveness checks. A return to normal

44
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.260
pressure does not erase the original deviation.
